\title{Which Marginals Feed the Machine?\\
Second-Moment Union Bounds, Exact Collision Marginals,\\
and an Unconditional Converse for Random Hashing}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Lower bounds on the probability of a union of events are commonly obtained by feeding two \emph{marginals} --- the probability of a single event and the probability of a pair of events --- into the second Bonferroni inequality. Applied to the failure event of a uniformly random hashing codebook, this route yields $\Prob[\text{failure}] \ge k/(2M)$ on $k$ competing messages and $M$ labels, but only in the regime $2(k-1)\le M$. We ask whether that regime restriction is a property of the marginals or of the inequality, and prove that it is a property of the inequality.

We first establish, in exact counting form and for an arbitrary finite family of finite sets, the Chung--Erd\H{o}s (second-moment) inequality $(\sum_i |A_i|)^2 \le |\bigcup_i A_i| \cdot \sum_{i,j} |A_i \cap A_j|$, by double counting the multiplicity function and applying Cauchy--Schwarz. From it we derive an abstract \emph{marginal-profile theorem}: a family whose members each have measure exactly $1/m$ and whose distinct pairs each have measure at most $1/c$ satisfies $c\,k\,N \le m\,|\bigcup_i A_i|\,(c+m(k-1))$, with no restriction on the number $k$ of sets. We show the bound is attained by a constant family, hence unimprovable as a function of the profile $(m,c,k,N)$, and that the Bonferroni-shaped conclusion $|\bigcup_i A_i|\ge kN/(2m)$ \emph{fails} for that same family, so the pairwise hypothesis is load-bearing.

Instantiating with the two hashing marginals gives $\Prob[\text{failure}]\ge k/(M+k-1)$ unconditionally; this dominates $k/(2M)$ throughout the Bonferroni regime $k\le M+1$, and yields a converse to Shannon's random-coding bound: for $k \ge M$ a uniformly random codebook fails with probability strictly greater than $1/2$. We then compute all relevant marginals exactly. A \emph{component law} states that the number of codebooks realising a prescribed collision pattern equals $M$ raised to the number of connected components of the pattern graph; consequently a prescribed star of $t$ collisions has probability exactly $M^{-t}$, the pairwise input is an equality, and vertex-sharing and vertex-disjoint pairs of collisions have the same marginal. A \emph{conditional marginal principle} yields the exact failure law $\Prob[\text{failure}] = 1-(1-1/M)^k$, whence the Shannon bound $k/M$, the matching lower bound $k/(M+k)$, and the hierarchy $k/(2M)\le k/(M+k-1)\le \Prob[\text{failure}] \le k/M$. Finally, averaging the exact law rather than the union bound produces a derandomisation that is never vacuous.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Let $\alpha$ be a finite alphabet of messages and $M \ge 1$ a number of labels. A \emph{codebook} is a function $H:\alpha \to \mathbb{Z}/M$; there are $M^{|\alpha|}$ codebooks, and ``a uniformly random codebook'' means the uniform measure on this finite set, equivalently independent uniform labels for distinct messages.

Fix a \emph{typical set} $S \subseteq \alpha$ and a transmitted message $x \in S$. The codebook fails at $x$ if some other typical message shares its label:
\[
  \fail(S,x) = \bigl\{\, H : \exists\, y \in S\setminus\{x\},\ H(y) = H(x) \,\bigr\}.
\]
Writing $k = |S\setminus\{x\}|$ for the number of competitors, Shannon's random-coding (union) bound gives $\Prob[\fail]\le k/M$: compression with $M \gg k$ labels succeeds with high probability.

The converse direction is standardly obtained from the \emph{second Bonferroni inequality}, which in exact counting form reads
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bonf}
  \sum_{i \in I} |A_i| \;\le\; \Bigl|\bigcup_{i\in I} A_i\Bigr| \;+\; \sum_{(i,j) \in I^{\offdiag}} |A_i \cap A_j|
\end{equation}
for an arbitrary finite family $(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of finite sets. Feeding \eqref{eq:bonf} the two hashing marginals --- a single collision has probability exactly $1/M$, and two collisions through a common vertex have probability at most $1/M^2$ --- produces
\begin{equation}\label{eq:oldbound}
  \Prob[\fail] \;\ge\; \frac{k}{2M}, \qquad \text{valid only when } 2(k-1)\le M .
\end{equation}
The side condition restricts the conclusion to precisely the regime in which it is weak, and disappears exactly where a converse would be interesting, as $k$ approaches and exceeds $M$.

\subsection{The question, and the answer}

Inequality \eqref{eq:bonf} holds for an arbitrary finite family, so the hypothesis $2(k-1)\le M$ must come either from the marginals fed in or from the algebraic shape of \eqref{eq:bonf}. We answer:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Is the regime restriction a property of the marginals, or of the machinery?} \emph{Answer: of the machinery.}
\end{quote}

The identical two marginals, fed into the second-moment (Chung--Erd\H{o}s) inequality instead of into \eqref{eq:bonf}, yield
\begin{equation}\label{eq:newbound}
  \Prob[\fail] \;\ge\; \frac{k}{M+k-1} \qquad \text{for all } k \text{ and all } M \ge 1,
\end{equation}
which dominates \eqref{eq:oldbound} throughout the regime where the latter is valid and remains greater than $1/2$ when $k \ge M$. Sections~\ref{sec:patterns}--\ref{sec:exact} show that no sharpening of the inputs was available: the second marginal is an equality, and the failure probability can in fact be computed exactly.

\section{Setting and notation}

Throughout $\iota$ and $\alpha$ denote finite message sets, $M \ge 1$ is the number of labels, and codebooks map into $\mathbb{Z}/M$. All probabilities are ratios of cardinalities with denominator $M^{|\alpha|}$; all statements are proved as exact statements about integers before being divided.

\begin{definition}[Collision event]
For $p,q \in \iota$, $C(p,q) = \{H : \iota \to \mathbb{Z}/M \mid H(p) = H(q)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Star event]
For $T \subseteq \iota$ and $x \in \iota$, $\Star(T,x) = \{H \mid H(y) = H(x) \text{ for all } y \in T\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Collision pattern and pattern event]
A \emph{collision pattern} is a finite set $P \subseteq \iota\times\iota$ of pairs, i.e. a graph on $\iota$. Its \emph{pattern event} is $E(P) = \{H \mid H(a) = H(b) \text{ for all } (a,b)\in P\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Failure event and bad strings]
For $S \subseteq \alpha$ and $x \in S$,
\[
  \fail(S,x) = \{H \mid \exists\, y \in S\setminus\{x\},\ H(y)=H(x)\},\qquad
  \bad(S,H) = \{x \in S \mid \exists\, y \in S\setminus\{x\},\ H(y)=H(x)\}.
\]
In particular
\begin{equation}\label{eq:failunion}
  \fail(S,x) \;=\; \bigcup_{y \in S\setminus\{x\}} C(y,x).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[No-collision event]
For $D \subseteq \iota$, $x \in \iota$: $\NC(D,x) = \{H \mid H(y)\ne H(x) \text{ for all } y \in D\}$.
\end{definition}

\noindent For a finite index set $I$ we write $I^{\offdiag} = \{(i,j)\in I\times I : i \ne j\}$, of size $|I|^2-|I|$.

\section{The second-moment machinery}\label{sec:machinery}

\subsection{The Chung--Erd\H{o}s inequality in counting form}

\begin{theorem}[Chung--Erd\H{o}s, exact counting form]\label{thm:ce}
Let $\Omega$ be a finite set, $I$ a finite index set and $A : I \to \mathcal{P}(\Omega)$ a family of finite subsets. Then
\[
  \Bigl(\sum_{i \in I} |A_i|\Bigr)^{2} \;\le\; \Bigl|\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\Bigr| \cdot \sum_{(i,j) \in I \times I} |A_i \cap A_j| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $U = \bigcup_{i\in I} A_i$ and define the \emph{multiplicity function} $f(w) = \#\{i \in I : w \in A_i\}$. Since $A_i \subseteq U$ for each $i$, we may write $|A_i| = \sum_{w\in U}\mathbf 1[w\in A_i]$, and exchanging summation gives the first-moment identity $\sum_{i\in I}|A_i| = \sum_{w\in U} f(w)$. Similarly $|A_i\cap A_j| = \sum_{w\in U}\mathbf 1[w\in A_i]\mathbf 1[w\in A_j]$, and exchanging summation over $I\times I$ gives the second-moment identity
\[
  \sum_{(i,j)\in I\times I}|A_i\cap A_j| \;=\; \sum_{w\in U}\Bigl(\sum_{i\in I}\mathbf 1[w\in A_i]\Bigr)^2 \;=\; \sum_{w\in U} f(w)^2 .
\]
The claim is Cauchy--Schwarz for $f$ against the constant function $1$ on $U$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ce} is the second-moment counterpart of \eqref{eq:bonf}: both consume exactly the data $\sum_i|A_i|$ and $\sum_{i\ne j}|A_i\cap A_j|$ and output a lower bound on $|U|$. Bonferroni bounds the overcount linearly, Chung--Erd\H{o}s quadratically; the difference in behaviour at large $k$ is exactly the difference between the two shapes.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The marginal-profile theorem}

\begin{definition}[Marginal profile]
Let $N \ge 1$, let $I$ be finite with $|I| = k$ and $A : I \to \mathcal{P}(\Omega)$. The family has \emph{first marginal exactly $1/m$} if $m|A_i| = N$ for all $i$, and \emph{second marginal at most $1/c$} if $c|A_i\cap A_j| \le N$ for all $(i,j)\in I^{\offdiag}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Marginal-profile lower bound]\label{thm:profile}
Let $A : I \to \mathcal{P}(\Omega)$ have first marginal exactly $1/m$ and second marginal at most $1/c$ relative to $N \ge 1$, with $k = |I|$ and $U = \bigcup_{i\in I}A_i$. Then
\[
  c\,k\,N \;\le\; m\,|U|\,\bigl(c + m(k-1)\bigr),
\]
equivalently, for $m,c \ge 1$,
\[
  \frac{|U|}{N} \;\ge\; \frac{c\,k}{m\,(c+m(k-1))} \;=\; \frac{k}{m + m^2(k-1)/c} .
\]
There is no restriction relating $k$, $m$ and $c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume $k \ge 1$ (the case $k=0$ is trivial) and set $T = \sum_{(i,j)\in I\times I}|A_i\cap A_j|$.

\emph{First moment.} From $m|A_i| = N$ we get
\begin{equation}\label{eq:first}
  m\sum_{i\in I}|A_i| = kN .
\end{equation}

\emph{Second moment.} Splitting $I\times I$ into diagonal and off-diagonal and using $A_i\cap A_i = A_i$,
\[
  T = \sum_{i\in I}|A_i| + \sum_{(i,j)\in I^{\offdiag}}|A_i\cap A_j| .
\]
The off-diagonal sum has $k^2-k$ terms, each at most $N/c$; multiplying the split identity by $mc$ and using \eqref{eq:first} on the diagonal term,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:second}
  mc\,T \;\le\; c\,(kN) + m(k^2-k)N .
\end{equation}

\emph{Combination.} By \eqref{eq:first}, Theorem~\ref{thm:ce} scaled by $m^2c$, and \eqref{eq:second},
\[
  c(kN)^2 = m^2c\Bigl(\sum_i|A_i|\Bigr)^2 \le m^2c\,|U|\,T = m|U|\cdot (mc\,T) \le m|U|\bigl(c\,kN + m(k^2-k)N\bigr).
\]
Since $k^2-k = k(k-1)$, the right side is $(kN)\cdot m|U|(c+m(k-1))$ and the left is $(kN)\cdot ckN$; cancel the positive factor $kN$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Sharpness and necessity of the pairwise input}

\begin{theorem}[The marginal bound is attained]\label{thm:sharp}
Take $\Omega$ of size $N = 2$, $m = c = 2$, and $A_1 = A_2 = A_3$ all equal to a fixed singleton, so $k = 3$. This family has first marginal exactly $1/2$ and second marginal at most $1/2$, and the inequality of Theorem~\ref{thm:profile} holds with equality: both sides equal $12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|A_i| = 1$, so $m|A_i| = 2 = N$; each pairwise intersection is the same singleton, so $c|A_i\cap A_j| = 2 \le N$; and $|U| = 1$. Then $ckN = 2\cdot3\cdot2 = 12$ and $m|U|(c+m(k-1)) = 2\cdot1\cdot(2+4) = 12$.
\end{proof}

Consequently Theorem~\ref{thm:profile} cannot be improved as a function of $(m,c,k,N)$ alone.

\begin{theorem}[The second marginal is load-bearing]\label{thm:necessary}
The Bonferroni-shaped conclusion $|U| \ge kN/(2m)$ --- the shape of \eqref{eq:oldbound} --- is false for a family with a perfect first marginal but no pairwise control: for the family of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} one has $2m|U| = 4 < 6 = kN$.
\end{theorem}

Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp} and~\ref{thm:necessary} bracket the situation: no better conclusion can be drawn from the two marginals, and dropping the pairwise marginal invalidates conclusions of this strength altogether.

\subsection{The unconditional hashing bound}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional lower bound for random hashing]\label{thm:hash}
Let $S\subseteq\alpha$, $x \in S$, $M \ge 1$ and $k = |S\setminus\{x\}|$. Then
\[
  k\,M^{|\alpha|} \;\le\; |\fail(S,x)|\,(M+k-1), \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \Prob[\fail] \ge \frac{k}{M+k-1},
\]
with no restriction relating $k$ and $M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:profile} to $A_y = C(y,x)$ for $y \in S\setminus\{x\}$, whose union is $\fail(S,x)$ by \eqref{eq:failunion}, with $N = M^{|\alpha|}$, $m = M$ (exact first marginal) and $c = M^2$ (pairwise marginal). This gives
\[
  M^2 k N \le M|\fail|\bigl(M^2 + M(k-1)\bigr) = M^2\bigl(|\fail|(M+k-1)\bigr),
\]
and cancelling $M^2>0$ finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Domination of the Bonferroni bound]\label{thm:dominate}
For $M \ge 1$ and $k \le M+1$, $\dfrac{k}{2M} \le \dfrac{k}{M+k-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$k \le M+1$ gives $M+k-1 \le 2M$, and both denominators are positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A converse above the pigeonhole rate]\label{thm:converse}
If $M \le k$ then $\Prob[\fail] > 1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:hash} it suffices that $k/(M+k-1) > 1/2$, i.e. $k > M-1$.
\end{proof}

The Bonferroni route can say nothing here: its hypothesis fails and its nominal value $k/(2M)$ can exceed $1$. The second-moment route, on \emph{the same two marginals}, shows a uniformly random codebook is more likely to fail than to succeed once the typical set has at least $M$ competitors.

\section{Exact marginals of arbitrary collision patterns}\label{sec:patterns}

\subsection{Collapse maps}

\begin{definition}[Collapse event]
For $f:\iota\to\iota$, $\Col(f) = \{H \mid H(f(a)) = H(a)\ \text{for all } a\}$, the codebooks constant on the fibres of $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact marginal of a collapse map]\label{thm:collapse}
If $f$ is idempotent ($f\circ f = f$) then $|\Col(f)| = M^{|f(\iota)|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Idempotence makes every point of the image a fixed point. The restriction map $H \mapsto H|_{f(\iota)}$ is a bijection $\Col(f) \to (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{f(\iota)}$: injective since $H(a) = H(f(a))$ recovers $H$; surjective since for $g$ on the image, $H(a) = g(f(a))$ lies in $\Col(f)$ (using $f(f(a)) = f(a)$) and restricts to $g$ (using $f(s) = s$ on the image).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pattern marginal via a collapse presentation]
Let $P$ be a pattern and $f$ idempotent with $f(a) = f(b)$ for every $(a,b)\in P$, and with every $H \in E(P)$ constant on the fibres of $f$. Then $|E(P)| = M^{|f(\iota)|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The second hypothesis gives $E(P)\subseteq\Col(f)$; the first gives the reverse, since $H\in\Col(f)$ and $(a,b)\in P$ imply $H(a) = H(f(a)) = H(f(b)) = H(b)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The star marginal}

\begin{theorem}[Exact star marginal]\label{thm:star}
If $T \subseteq \iota$ and $x \notin T$, then $|\Star(T,x)| = M^{|\iota|-|T|}$, i.e.\ $\Prob[\Star(T,x)] = M^{-|T|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $f(a) = x$ for $a\in T$ and $f(a)=a$ otherwise. Since $x\notin T$, $f$ is idempotent with image $\iota\setminus T$ of size $|\iota|-|T|$, and $\Star(T,x) = \Col(f)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[First marginal, exactly]
For $p \ne q$, $|C(p,q)| = M^{|\iota|-1}$: a prescribed collision has probability exactly $1/M$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The second marginal is an equality]\label{cor:secondeq}
For distinct $p,q,r$: $M^{2}\,|C(p,r)\cap C(q,r)| = M^{|\iota|}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$C(p,r)\cap C(q,r) = \Star(\{p,q\},r)$ with $r\notin\{p,q\}$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:star}.
\end{proof}

So the inequality fed into both routes loses nothing: no refinement of the inputs could have removed the regime restriction.

\begin{theorem}[Two disjoint collisions are exactly independent]
Let $p\ne q$, $r \ne s$, $p\ne r$, $p\ne s$, $q\ne r$. Then $M^{2}\,|C(p,q)\cap C(r,s)| = M^{|\iota|}$. (Here $q = s$ is permitted.)
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $f(a) = q$ if $a = p$, $f(a) = s$ if $a = r$, and $f(a) = a$ otherwise. The hypotheses make $f$ idempotent with image $\iota\setminus\{p,r\}$, and $C(p,q)\cap C(r,s) = \Col(f)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The component law}

\begin{definition}
Let $\sim_P$ be the equivalence relation on $\iota$ generated by the adjacency relation of $P$; its classes are the connected components of the pattern graph, isolated vertices included. Write $c(P)$ for their number.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:const}
$H \in E(P)$ if and only if $H$ is constant on each $\sim_P$-class.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Constancy on classes implies edge-respect. Conversely, if $H$ respects every edge, then $\{(a,b) : H(a) = H(b)\}$ is an equivalence relation containing the adjacency relation of $P$, hence contains $\sim_P$; formally, induct over the reflexive, symmetric and transitive generation steps.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The component law]\label{thm:component}
For an arbitrary collision pattern $P$ on a finite $\iota$, $|E(P)| = M^{c(P)}$; equivalently, a uniformly random codebook realises all collisions of $P$ with probability $M^{-(|\iota|-c(P))}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:const}, $E(P)$ is in bijection with the functions from the quotient $\iota/\!\sim_P$ to the label set: forwards, pass to the induced function on classes; backwards, compose with the quotient projection. There are $M^{c(P)}$ such functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Component count of a star, from two enumerations]
Let $T\subseteq\iota$, $x \notin T$, and let $P$ join every $y\in T$ to $x$. If $M \ge 2$ then $c(P) = |\iota|-|T|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:component} gives $|E(P)| = M^{c(P)}$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:star} gives $|E(P)| = M^{|\iota|-|T|}$; for $M\ge2$ the map $n\mapsto M^n$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The marginals available to any inclusion--exclusion-type machinery here are $M^{-(|\iota|-c(P))}$: they depend on the pattern only through its component count and are blind to its shape. Two collisions always cost $M^{-2}$, whether or not they share a vertex. The regime restriction in \eqref{eq:oldbound} can therefore be attributed neither to coarse inputs nor to hidden dependence; it belongs to the inequality.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact failure law}\label{sec:exact}

\begin{definition}[Unconstrained at a coordinate]
An event $G$ of codebooks is \emph{unconstrained at $y$} if for every $H \in G$ and every label $v$, the codebook agreeing with $H$ off $y$ and taking value $v$ at $y$ also lies in $G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional collision marginal]\label{thm:condmarg}
If $G$ is unconstrained at $y$ and $y \ne x$, then $M\,|G\cap C(y,x)| = |G|$, i.e. $\Prob[C(y,x)\mid G] = 1/M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Exhibit a bijection $(G\cap C(y,x))\times \mathbb{Z}/M \to G$, $(K,v)\mapsto K^{y\mapsto v}$ (overwrite the $y$-th label by $v$). It lands in $G$ since $G$ is unconstrained at $y$. Injectivity: from the image one reads $v$ at $y$ and all other coordinates of $K$ directly, while $K(y) = K(x)$ is an off-$y$ coordinate because $K \in C(y,x)$ and $y \ne x$. Surjectivity: given $H\in G$ take $K = H^{y\mapsto H(x)}$, which lies in $G$ and in $C(y,x)$, and $v = H(y)$. Counting gives the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:uncon}
If $y \notin D$ and $y \ne x$ then $\NC(D,x)$ is unconstrained at $y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[One competitor at a time]\label{lem:step}
If $y\notin D$ and $y \ne x$ then $M\,|\NC(D\cup\{y\},x)| = (M-1)\,|\NC(D,x)|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
With $G = \NC(D,x)$ we have $\NC(D\cup\{y\},x) = G\setminus(G\cap C(y,x))$, of size $|G| - |G\cap C(y,x)|$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:uncon} and Theorem~\ref{thm:condmarg}, $|G\cap C(y,x)| = |G|/M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact survival count]\label{thm:survival}
For $x\notin D$ and $k = |D|$: $\;M^{k}\,|\NC(D,x)| = (M-1)^{k}\,M^{|\iota|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $D$: the empty case is the whole space; the step is Lemma~\ref{lem:step} multiplied by $M^{|D|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact failure law]\label{thm:exactlaw}
For $S\subseteq\alpha$, $x\in S$, $k = |S\setminus\{x\}|$ and $M \ge 1$,
\[
  M^{k}|\fail(S,x)| + (M-1)^{k}M^{|\alpha|} = M^{k}M^{|\alpha|}, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \Prob[\fail] = 1 - \Bigl(1-\frac1M\Bigr)^{k}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The failure event is the complement of $\NC(S\setminus\{x\},x)$, so their cardinalities sum to $M^{|\alpha|}$. Multiply by $M^k$ and substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:survival}.
\end{proof}

Brute-force enumeration confirms the law in small cases: for $|\alpha| = 3$, $M = 2$ the failure count is $2^3 - 1^2\cdot 2 = 6$ out of $8$; for $|\alpha| = 4$, $M = 3$ it is $3^4 - 2^3\cdot 3 = 57$ out of $81$.

\begin{corollary}[Shannon bound from the exact law]\label{cor:shannon}
$\Prob[\fail] \le k/M$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Bernoulli's inequality with $t = -1/M \ge -1$ gives $1-k/M \le (1-1/M)^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Matching lower bound]\label{cor:harmonic}
$\Prob[\fail] \ge \dfrac{k}{M+k}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Bernoulli with $t = 1/M$ gives $1+k/M \le (1+1/M)^k$. Multiplying by $(1-1/M)^k \ge 0$ and using $\bigl((1+1/M)(1-1/M)\bigr)^k = (1-1/M^2)^k \le 1$ yields $(1+k/M)(1-1/M)^k \le 1$, i.e. $(1-1/M)^k \le M/(M+k)$; now apply Theorem~\ref{thm:exactlaw}.
\end{proof}

Together, $k/(M+k) \le \Prob[\fail] \le k/M$ for all $k$ and all $M \ge 1$: random hashing fails with probability $\Theta(\min(1,k/M))$, with no regime hypotheses.

\section{The hierarchy of marginal-driven bounds}

\begin{lemma}[Integer Bernoulli]\label{lem:intbern}
For $M \ge 1$ and $j \ge 0$: $(M-1)^{j}(M+j) \le M^{\,j+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $j$; both sides equal $M$ at $j = 0$. For the step use $(M-1)(M+j+1) \le M(M+j)$, i.e.\ $-j-1 \le 0$; multiplying the inductive hypothesis by this and cancelling the positive factor $M+j$ gives $(M-1)^{j+1}(M+j+1) \le M^{j+2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The exact law implies the second-moment bound]\label{thm:smle}
For $M \ge 1$, $k \ge 0$: $\;\dfrac{k}{M+k-1} \le 1 - \Bigl(1-\dfrac1M\Bigr)^{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $k = 0$ both sides vanish; otherwise write $k = j+1$. Lemma~\ref{lem:intbern} multiplied by $M-1\ge0$ gives $(M-1)^{j+1}(M+j) \le (M-1)M^{j+1}$, i.e.
\[
  \Bigl(1-\frac1M\Bigr)^{j+1} = \frac{(M-1)^{j+1}}{M^{j+1}} \le \frac{M-1}{M+j} = 1 - \frac{j+1}{M+j},
\]
and $M+k-1 = M+j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full hierarchy]\label{thm:hierarchy}
For every $S$, every $x \in S$, every $M \ge 1$ and every $k = |S\setminus\{x\}| \le M+1$,
\[
  \frac{k}{2M} \;\le\; \frac{k}{M+k-1} \;\le\; \Prob[\fail] \;=\; 1-\Bigl(1-\frac1M\Bigr)^{k} \;\le\; \frac{k}{M}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dominate}, then Theorem~\ref{thm:smle} with Theorem~\ref{thm:exactlaw}, then Corollary~\ref{cor:shannon}.
\end{proof}

The classical Bonferroni output is thus the weakest member of a four-term chain whose middle terms use only the first two marginals; only the leftmost inequality needs $k \le M+1$.

\section{Exact derandomisation}

\begin{lemma}[Incidence double count]\label{lem:incidence}
$\displaystyle\sum_{H} |\bad(S,H)| = \sum_{x\in S} |\fail(S,x)|$, the left sum over all $M^{|\alpha|}$ codebooks.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both sides count incidences $(H,x)$ with $x \in S$ lost by $H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact derandomisation]\label{thm:derand}
Let $S\subseteq\alpha$, $M \ge 1$ and $k = |S|-1$. There exists a codebook $H$ with
\[
  M^{k}\,|\bad(S,H)| \;\le\; |S|\,\bigl(M^{k}-(M-1)^{k}\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $x\in S$ we have $|S\setminus\{x\}| = k$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:exactlaw} gives $M^k|\fail(S,x)| = M^{|\alpha|}(M^k-(M-1)^k)$. Summing over $x\in S$ and using Lemma~\ref{lem:incidence},
\[
  M^{k}\sum_{H}|\bad(S,H)| = |S|M^{|\alpha|}\bigl(M^k-(M-1)^k\bigr).
\]
Choosing $H_0$ minimising $|\bad(S,H)|$ gives $M^{|\alpha|}|\bad(S,H_0)| \le \sum_H |\bad(S,H)|$; substitute and cancel $M^{|\alpha|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fractional form]\label{cor:frac}
If $S \ne \varnothing$ and $M \ge 1$, some codebook $H$ satisfies
$\dfrac{|\bad(S,H)|}{|S|} \le 1-\Bigl(1-\dfrac1M\Bigr)^{|S|-1}$.
\end{corollary}

For $M \ge 2$ the right-hand side is strictly less than $1$ for every $S$: the statement is never vacuous, however large the typical set. The classical union-bound derandomisation, giving a codebook losing at most $|S|(|S|-1)/M$ messages, is vacuous once $|S|-1\ge M$; and the new bound always implies it:

\begin{theorem}[The exact bound dominates the union bound]\label{thm:powsub}
For all $M,k \ge 0$: $\;M^{k} \le (M-1)^{k} + kM^{k-1}$, i.e.\ $M^k-(M-1)^k \le kM^{k-1}$, i.e.\ $1-(1-1/M)^k \le k/M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. Assuming $M^n \le (M-1)^n+nM^{n-1}$ and multiplying by $M$,
\[
  M^{n+1} \le M(M-1)^n + nM^{n} \le (M-1)^{n+1} + (n+1)M^{n},
\]
where the last step uses $M(M-1)^n \le (M-1)^{n+1}+M^n$, itself a consequence of $(M-1)^n \le M^n$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and applications}

\subsection*{Algorithmic content}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item \textbf{Marginal-profile evaluation.} Given $A_1,\dots,A_k$ over a universe of size $N$ as bit-vectors, compute $m = N/|A_1|$ and $c = \min_{i\ne j}\lfloor N/|A_i\cap A_j|\rfloor$, and output the certified bound $ck/(m(c+m(k-1)))$ on $|U|/N$. Cost $O(k^2N/w)$ word operations; by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} the bound is unimprovable from the profile alone.
\item \textbf{Component-law marginal computation.} Given a pattern $P$ on $n$ vertices, compute $c(P)$ by union--find in $O((n+|P|)\alpha(n))$ time and output $\Prob[E(P)] = M^{-(n-c(P))}$ exactly (Theorem~\ref{thm:component}), replacing any enumeration over $M^n$ codebooks.
\item \textbf{Greedy exact derandomisation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:derand} asserts existence; the method of conditional expectations turns it into an algorithm, labelling the messages of $S$ one at a time and choosing at each step the label minimising the conditional expectation of $|\bad(S,H)|$, computable in closed form from current bin occupancies by Theorem~\ref{thm:condmarg}. Cost $O(|S|M)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Applications}

Theorem~\ref{thm:converse} supplies a converse for source coding by random binning: above the pigeonhole rate failure has probability exceeding $1/2$, so with the achievability bound $k/M$ the operational threshold is pinned at $k \asymp M$ from both sides. In load balancing and hash-table analysis, Theorem~\ref{thm:exactlaw} is the exact collision probability for a designated key and Corollaries~\ref{cor:shannon}--\ref{cor:harmonic} sandwich it without assumptions, while Corollary~\ref{cor:frac} gives an existence statement for a fixed hash function with a bounded fraction of colliding keys. More generally Theorem~\ref{thm:profile} applies wherever a first and second marginal are available and the Bonferroni route stalls --- in particular past the point where the number of events exceeds the inverse first marginal.

\section{Discussion and future work}

\subsection*{What was established}

The regime restriction $2(k-1)\le M$ is an artefact of the second Bonferroni inequality, not of the collision marginals. Three lines of evidence: (i) the same two marginals fed to Chung--Erd\H{o}s give an unconditional bound (Theorem~\ref{thm:hash}) dominating the Bonferroni bound wherever the latter applies (Theorem~\ref{thm:dominate}) and remaining informative above the pigeonhole rate (Theorem~\ref{thm:converse}); (ii) the marginals contain no slack --- the pairwise input is an equality (Corollary~\ref{cor:secondeq}) and all pattern marginals are given exactly by the component law (Theorem~\ref{thm:component}); (iii) the abstract theorem is attained (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}) while its pairwise hypothesis cannot be dropped (Theorem~\ref{thm:necessary}). Quantitatively, the exact law (Theorem~\ref{thm:exactlaw}) settles the question, sandwiches the failure probability between $k/(M+k)$ and $k/M$, places the classical bound at the bottom of a four-term hierarchy (Theorem~\ref{thm:hierarchy}), and yields a never-vacuous derandomisation (Theorem~\ref{thm:derand}).

\subsection*{Limitations}

The exact law needs full independence of labels across messages, since the survival recursion requires the no-collision event to be unconstrained at each fresh coordinate. Under $t$-wise independent hash families only the first $t$ marginals are guaranteed --- which is exactly why Theorem~\ref{thm:profile}, using only two, matters: Theorem~\ref{thm:hash} requires pairwise independence only and applies verbatim to universal hash families, whereas the exact law does not.

\subsection*{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item \textbf{Higher-order profiles.} What is the optimal union lower bound from the first $t$ marginals, as a function of the profile alone? For $t=2$ the answer is Theorem~\ref{thm:profile}. In general this is a moment problem over multiplicity distributions on $\{0,\dots,k\}$ with prescribed first $t$ moments, and its solution would quantify the exact strength of $t$-wise independence here.
\item \textbf{Non-uniform profiles.} Index-dependent $m_i$ and $c_{ij}$ give a weighted Cauchy--Schwarz problem; identifying the extremal configurations extends the analysis to non-uniform sources.
\item \textbf{Beyond stars.} With all marginals available through the component law, using more than two of them --- in a higher Bonferroni expansion or a local-lemma argument on the pattern graph --- may capture regimes where the second-moment bound is still lossy.
\item \textbf{Algorithmic derandomisation guarantees.} A fully rigorous conditional-expectation derandomisation with exact accounting of the loss would give a deterministic construction matching Corollary~\ref{cor:frac}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary of principal results}

